{"name":"Maui","hex":"#21a5be","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Maui","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The second-largest island of Hawaii.","Alternative spelling of Māui."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A barangay of Delfin Albano, Isabela, Philippines.","A barangay of Caramoran, Catanduanes, Philippines."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Maui"}],"entries":[{"title":"Maui","blocks":[{"text":"Maui is the second-largest island in the Hawaiian archipelago, at 727.2 square miles (1,883 km2). It is the 17th-largest in the United States. Maui is one of Maui County's four sizable islands, along with Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi, and Kahoʻolawe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Maui_Landsat_Photo.jpg/330px-Maui_Landsat_Photo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maui","wikidata":"Q188705"},{"title":"Maui (Moana)","blocks":[{"text":"Maui is a fictional character that appears in the 2016 Walt Disney Animation Studios animated film Moana. He was created by directors Ron Clements and John Musker and is voiced by American actor Dwayne Johnson. Maui is loosely based on the mythological figure Māui in Polynesian mythology. He is characterized as a demigod, trickster and shapeshifter and is distinguishable by his bulky tattooed body, thick, flowing hair and magical fish hook that allows him to shapeshift into various creatures. Maui's tattoos recount his deeds and feature an animated miniature version of himself that pokes fun at his overinflated ego. The character returns in the sequel film Moana 2, which premiered in November of 2024. Johnson also portrays Maui in the 2026 live-action remake of Moana."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/57/Maui_Disney.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maui_(Moana)","wikidata":"Q24455661"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/ISS-38_Hawaiian_Island_chain.jpg/500px-ISS-38_Hawaiian_Island_chain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/ISS-38_Hawaiian_Island_chain.jpg/1280px-ISS-38_Hawaiian_Island_chain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/ISS-38_Hawaiian_Island_chain.jpg/500px-ISS-38_Hawaiian_Island_chain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Maui (center right, with Molokaʻi, Lānaʻi, and Kahoʻolawe to its left) as seen from the International Space Station[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AISS-38_Hawaiian_Island_chain.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Maui2022OSM.png/500px-Maui2022OSM.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Maui2022OSM.png/1280px-Maui2022OSM.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Maui2022OSM.png/500px-Maui2022OSM.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Map of Maui and Kahoʻolawe","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaui2022OSM.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Mount_Haleakala_Crater_Maui_Hawaii_%2845740764101%29.jpg/500px-Mount_Haleakala_Crater_Maui_Hawaii_%2845740764101%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Mount_Haleakala_Crater_Maui_Hawaii_%2845740764101%29.jpg/1280px-Mount_Haleakala_Crater_Maui_Hawaii_%2845740764101%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https